<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-comparing-distributions">
  <title>Comparing Two Distributions</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Compare two distributions using histograms drawn on identical scales.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use a benchmark value to compare groups quantitatively.</p></li>
      <li><p>Write a comparative paragraph that emphasizes differences between groups.</p></li>
      <li><p>Distinguish an association observed in data from a causal claim.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>So far we have described one distribution at a time. Most interesting
    statistical questions involve a comparison: does this group differ from
    that one? Histograms answer such questions well, provided they are drawn
    carefully.</p>

    <p>Our descriptions still focus on the overall pattern<mdash/>shape,
    center, and spread<mdash/>and on deviations from it. But comparison adds a
    tool: because histograms make percentages easy to compute, we can compare
    specific intervals of values directly.</p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-birthweight-setup">
    <title>Smoking and birth weight</title>

    <p>Does smoking during pregnancy affect birth weight? To investigate,
    doctors collected data on <m>189</m> new mothers who gave birth at a
    hospital in Massachusetts during the <m>1980</m>s. Of these,
    <m>115</m> did not smoke during pregnancy and <m>74</m> did.</p>

    <table xml:id="table-birthweight">
      <title>Birth weights of babies born to <m>189</m> mothers, by smoking
      status</title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="medium">
        <row bottom="medium" header="yes">
          <cell>Birth weight (grams)</cell>
          <cell>Nonsmokers</cell>
          <cell>Smokers</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell><m>500</m> to <m>1000</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>1000</m> to <m>1500</m></cell><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>1500</m> to <m>2000</m></cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell><m>6</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>2000</m> to <m>2500</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell><cell><m>22</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>2500</m> to <m>3000</m></cell><cell><m>22</m></cell><cell><m>16</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>3000</m> to <m>3500</m></cell><cell><m>29</m></cell><cell><m>15</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>3500</m> to <m>4000</m></cell><cell><m>27</m></cell><cell><m>12</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>4000</m> to <m>4500</m></cell><cell><m>6</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>4500</m> to <m>5000</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell></row>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><m>\text{Total}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>115</m></cell>
          <cell><m>74</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>As always, left endpoints are included in the bin and right endpoints
    are not, so a baby weighing exactly <m>1000</m> grams belongs to the
    interval <m>1000</m> to <m>1500</m>.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-nonsmokers">
      <caption>Birth weights of babies born to the <m>115</m> mothers who did
      not smoke during pregnancy.</caption>
      <image width="85%" xml:id="image-nonsmokers">
        <description>A histogram of birth weights for babies of nonsmoking
        mothers. Bars rise gradually from zero at 500 grams to a peak of 29 in
        the bin from 3000 to 3500 grams, with a nearly equal bar of 27 next to
        it, then drop off quickly. The distribution leans slightly to the
        left, with a thin tail toward the low weights.</description>
        <asymptote>
unitsize(0.0017cm, 0.115cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));

real
[] L = {500,1000,1500,2000,2500,3000,3500,4000,4500};
real
[] C = {  0,   3,   8,  18,  22,  29,  27,   6,   2};

for (int i = 0; i &lt; L.length; ++i) {
  if (C
[i] &gt; 0) {
    filldraw((L
[i],0)--(L
[i]+500,0)--(L
[i]+500,C
[i])--(L
[i],C
[i])--cycle,
             paleblue, black+0.4bp);
  }
}

draw((500,0)--(5250,0), black+0.6bp);
for (real t = 500; t &lt;= 5000; t += 500) {
  draw((t,0)--(t,-0.8), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", t), (t,-1.1), S);
}

draw((500,0)--(500,32), black+0.6bp);
for (real s = 0; s &lt;= 30; s += 5) {
  draw((500,s)--(420,s), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", s), (380,s), W);
}

label("Birth weight (grams)", (2750,-4.2));
label(rotate(90)*"Number of babies", (-420,16));
label("Nonsmokers ($n = 115$)", (4100,29));
        </asymptote>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-smokers">
      <caption>Birth weights of babies born to the <m>74</m> mothers who
      smoked during pregnancy. The horizontal scale, the bin width, and the
      vertical scale all match <xref ref="fig-nonsmokers"/>.</caption>
      <image width="85%" xml:id="image-smokers">
        <description>A histogram of birth weights for babies of smoking
        mothers, drawn on exactly the same axes as the previous figure. The
        peak of 22 occurs in the bin from 2000 to 2500 grams, noticeably
        further left than the nonsmokers' peak, and the bars fall away toward
        the heavier weights with almost nothing above 4000 grams.</description>
        <asymptote>
unitsize(0.0017cm, 0.115cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));

real
[] L = {500,1000,1500,2000,2500,3000,3500,4000,4500};
real
[] C = {  1,   1,   6,  22,  16,  15,  12,   1,   0};

for (int i = 0; i &lt; L.length; ++i) {
  if (C
[i] &gt; 0) {
    filldraw((L
[i],0)--(L
[i]+500,0)--(L
[i]+500,C
[i])--(L
[i],C
[i])--cycle,
             palered, black+0.4bp);
  }
}

draw((500,0)--(5250,0), black+0.6bp);
for (real t = 500; t &lt;= 5000; t += 500) {
  draw((t,0)--(t,-0.8), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", t), (t,-1.1), S);
}

draw((500,0)--(500,32), black+0.6bp);
for (real s = 0; s &lt;= 30; s += 5) {
  draw((500,s)--(420,s), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", s), (380,s), W);
}

label("Birth weight (grams)", (2750,-4.2));
label(rotate(90)*"Number of babies", (-420,16));
label("Smokers ($n = 74$)", (4100,29));
        </asymptote>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <assemblage xml:id="asm-same-scales">
      <title>Why the scales must match</title>
      <p>Both histograms use the same horizontal range, the same bin width,
      and the same vertical scale. This is not decoration<mdash/>it is what
      makes the comparison valid.</p>
      <p>If the two graphs used different bin widths, differences in the bars
      might be artifacts of the binning rather than differences in the babies.
      If they used different vertical scales, a taller bar in one graph would
      not mean more babies. Matching all three lets you compare the pictures
      directly.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <!-- READING CHECK 1 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-same-scales">
      <statement>
        <p>Why is it important that <xref ref="fig-nonsmokers"/> and
        <xref ref="fig-smokers"/> use identical axes?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>So that differences you see between the two pictures reflect
            differences in the data rather than differences in how the graphs
            were drawn.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. With matched scales and bin widths, a visual
            difference is a real difference.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>So that the two groups have the same number of individuals.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The groups have different sizes<mdash/><m>115</m> and
            <m>74</m><mdash/>and matching the axes does not change that.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Because histograms of the same variable are required to use the
            same bins.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>No such requirement exists in general. It is a deliberate
            choice made here because we intend to compare.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>So the percentages in each bin will be equal.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The percentages are not equal and are not expected to be.
            Finding where they differ is the whole point.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-birthweight-observations">
    <title>Shape, center, and spread for each group</title>

    <p><em>Nonsmokers.</em> The distribution of birth weights appears skewed
    slightly to the left. Weights for this group fall between approximately
    <m>1000</m> and <m>5000</m> grams, an overall range of about
    <m>4000</m> grams. Nearly half of these babies weighed between
    <m>3000</m> and <m>4000</m> grams:</p>

    <me>\frac{29 + 27}{115} = \frac{56}{115} \approx 0.487 = 48.7\%</me>

    <p>with fewer babies in the lower weight ranges.</p>

    <p><em>Smokers.</em> The distribution appears slightly skewed to the
    right. Weights fall between approximately <m>500</m> and <m>4500</m>
    grams, also an overall range of about <m>4000</m> grams. Nearly half of
    these babies weighed between <m>2000</m> and <m>3000</m> grams:</p>

    <me>\frac{22 + 16}{74} = \frac{38}{74} \approx 0.514 = 51.4\%</me>

    <p>with fewer babies in the heavier weight ranges.</p>

    <p>Two things are worth noticing. The overall ranges are
    <em>identical</em> at about <m>4000</m> grams, so spread alone does not
    distinguish the groups. What differs is <em>where</em> the bulk of each
    distribution sits: the nonsmokers' typical range is a full
    <m>1000</m> grams heavier.</p>

    <p>A caution carried over from the previous sections: because the choice
    of bin width affects the shape of a histogram, we cannot make precise
    statements about center and spread here. Our sense of the <q>typical</q>
    range is itself affected by the binning.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 2 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-range-same">
      <statement>
        <p>Both groups have an overall range of about <m>4000</m> grams. What
        does that tell you?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>The two groups are similarly variable, so range alone does not
            distinguish them<mdash/>the difference lies elsewhere.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. Equal ranges with different centers is exactly the
            pattern here: the smokers' distribution is shifted lower, not
            narrower.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The two distributions are essentially the same.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Range is only one feature. The peaks sit a full
            <m>1000</m> grams apart, which is a substantial difference.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Smoking has no effect on birth weight.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The ranges match, but the locations of the distributions do
            not. Look at where each peak falls.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The two groups must be the same size.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Range says nothing about group size. These groups contain
            <m>115</m> and <m>74</m> mothers.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-benchmarks">
    <title>Comparing with a benchmark</title>

    <p>Another strategy for comparing distributions is to pick a meaningful
    cutoff value<mdash/>a <term>benchmark</term><mdash/>and compute what
    percentage of each group falls beyond it. Benchmarks that come from
    outside the data, such as medical definitions, are especially persuasive
    because nobody can accuse you of choosing the cutoff to favor a
    conclusion.</p>

    <example xml:id="ex-low-birth-weight">
      <title>Low birth weight</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Doctors define <term>low birth weight</term> as a weight below
        <m>2500</m> grams. Compare the percentage of smokers and nonsmokers
        whose babies met this definition.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><em>Nonsmokers:</em> <m>3 + 8 + 18 = 29</m> babies weighed less
        than <m>2500</m> grams.</p>
        <me>\frac{29}{115} \approx 0.252 = 25.2\%</me>
        <p><em>Smokers:</em> <m>1 + 1 + 6 + 22 = 30</m> babies weighed less
        than <m>2500</m> grams.</p>
        <me>\frac{30}{74} \approx 0.405 = 40.5\%</me>
        <p>Note that the raw counts are nearly identical, <m>29</m> versus
        <m>30</m>, while the percentages differ enormously. This is why
        relative frequency matters when group sizes differ.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-macrosomia">
      <title>Macrosomia</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A condition called <term>macrosomia</term>, informally <q>big baby
        syndrome,</q> is defined as a birth weight of <m>4000</m> grams or
        more. Compare the two groups.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><em>Nonsmokers:</em> <m>6 + 2 = 8</m> babies weighed
        <m>4000</m> grams or more.</p>
        <me>\frac{8}{115} \approx 0.070 = 7.0\%</me>
        <p><em>Smokers:</em> only <m>1</m> baby weighed <m>4000</m> grams or
        more.</p>
        <me>\frac{1}{74} \approx 0.014 = 1.4\%</me>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- READING CHECK 3 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-counts-vs-percents">
      <statement>
        <p>In <xref ref="ex-low-birth-weight"/>, <m>29</m> nonsmokers and
        <m>30</m> smokers had low-birth-weight babies<mdash/>nearly the same
        count. Why is it misleading to stop there?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>The groups are different sizes, so the same count represents a
            much larger share of the smokers.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. Thirty out of <m>74</m> is a far bigger fraction than
            <m>29</m> out of <m>115</m><mdash/><m>40.5\%</m> versus
            <m>25.2\%</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The counts were measured with different bin widths.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Both groups use identical bins. The issue is group size, not
            binning.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Counts are always less accurate than percentages.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Counts are exact. The problem is that they are not
            <em>comparable</em> across groups of different sizes.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The benchmark of <m>2500</m> grams is arbitrary.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>It is a standard medical definition, not an arbitrary choice.
            And the issue here would remain at any cutoff.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 4 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-benchmark-compute">
      <statement>
        <p>Using <xref ref="table-birthweight"/>, approximately what
        percentage of babies born to nonsmokers weighed <m>3000</m> grams or
        more?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>About <m>55.7\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct: <m>29 + 27 + 6 + 2 = 64</m>, and
            <m>64 \div 115 \approx 0.557</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>48.7\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is the percentage between <m>3000</m> and
            <m>4000</m> grams. <q><m>3000</m> or more</q> also includes the
            two heaviest bins.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>64\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p><m>64</m> is the count, not the percentage. Divide by the group
            total of <m>115</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>33.9\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This divides by <m>189</m>, the total number of mothers in the
            study. When describing nonsmokers, the group is the
            <m>115</m> nonsmokers.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-comparative-paragraph">
    <title>Writing the comparison</title>

    <p>Now assemble everything into prose. The key difference from a
    single-group description: <em>emphasize the comparison</em>. A paragraph
    that describes group A fully, then describes group B fully, leaves the
    reader to do the comparing. Do it for them.</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>In this observational study, we compared mothers who smoked during
      pregnancy to mothers who did not. The variable is the birth weight of
      their babies.</p>

      <p>Both groups showed a lot of variability in birth weight, with
      identical overall range estimates of about <m>4000</m> grams, and there
      was substantial overlap between the distributions. Nonsmokers had babies
      weighing between approximately <m>1000</m> and <m>5000</m> grams;
      smokers had babies weighing between approximately <m>500</m> and
      <m>4500</m> grams.</p>

      <p>However, we observe important differences in the typical ranges for
      the two groups. For nonsmokers, nearly half the babies weighed between
      <m>3000</m> and <m>4000</m> grams (<m>56</m> of <m>115</m>, or
      <m>48.7\%</m>), with fewer babies in the lower ranges. For smokers,
      nearly half weighed between <m>2000</m> and <m>3000</m> grams
      (<m>38</m> of <m>74</m>, or <m>51.4\%</m>), with fewer babies in the
      heavier ranges.</p>

      <p>Using the medical definition of low birth weight, below
      <m>2500</m> grams, smokers in this study had a much higher incidence:
      <m>25.2\%</m> (<m>29</m> of <m>115</m>) of babies born to nonsmokers met
      the definition, compared to <m>40.5\%</m> (<m>30</m> of <m>74</m>) of
      babies born to smokers. In this study, smoking is associated with lower
      birth weights, though the variability in the data suggests that other
      variables also contribute to birth weight.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>Read that last sentence again. It says smoking is
    <em>associated with</em> lower birth weights<mdash/>not that smoking
    <em>causes</em> them. This is an observational study: nobody assigned
    mothers to smoke or not smoke. Mothers who smoke may differ from mothers
    who do not in many other ways that also affect birth weight. The data
    establishes a clear association, and stops there.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 5 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-association">
      <statement>
        <p>Based on this study, which conclusion is appropriate?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>In this group of <m>189</m> mothers, smoking during pregnancy
            was associated with lower birth weights.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. It describes an association, names the group studied,
            and claims nothing beyond what the data supports.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Smoking during pregnancy causes low birth weight.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This is an observational study, not an experiment. Mothers
            chose whether to smoke, and may differ in other ways that affect
            birth weight.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>If a mother smokes, her baby will have low birth weight.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Nearly <m>60\%</m> of the smokers' babies were <em>not</em> low
            birth weight. The association is about rates across a group, not a
            prediction for any individual.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Smoking has no relationship to birth weight, since both groups
            had the same range.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Equal ranges do not mean equal distributions. The low-birth-weight
            rate differs by more than <m>15</m> percentage points.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 6: short answer, recorded -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-write-comparison">
      <statement>
        <p>Using <xref ref="ex-macrosomia"/>, write two sentences comparing
        the rate of macrosomia between the two groups. Include both
        percentages, and be careful about what the comparison does and does
        not establish.</p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Something like: <q>Babies born to nonsmoking mothers were about
        five times as likely to meet the definition of macrosomia:
        <m>7.0\%</m> (<m>8</m> of <m>115</m>) compared to <m>1.4\%</m>
        (<m>1</m> of <m>74</m>) for smokers. However, this comparison rests on
        a single macrosomic baby in the smoking group, so the difference
        should be treated cautiously.</q></p>
        <p>The second sentence is what separates a good answer from an
        adequate one. A percentage built on a count of one is fragile<mdash/>if
        that single baby had weighed slightly less, the smokers' rate would be
        <m>0\%</m>. Reporting the underlying counts alongside the percentages
        lets a reader see this for themselves.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-dotplot-vs-histogram">
    <title>Dotplots and histograms compared</title>

    <p>We now have two graphs for the distribution of a quantitative variable.
    Neither is better in general; each highlights different aspects of the
    data.</p>

    <table xml:id="table-dotplot-histogram">
      <title>Choosing between a dotplot and a histogram</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="medium">
        <row bottom="medium" header="yes">
          <cell></cell>
          <cell>Dotplot</cell>
          <cell>Histogram</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Individual values</cell>
          <cell>visible</cell>
          <cell>not visible</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Large data sets</cell>
          <cell>becomes unreadable</cell>
          <cell>handles them well</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Shape, center, spread</cell>
          <cell>unaffected by construction</cell>
          <cell>affected by bin choice</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Overall range</cell>
          <cell>exact</cell>
          <cell>approximate</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell>Percentages</cell>
          <cell>tedious to compute</cell>
          <cell>easy to compute</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>There are no rules dictating which to use. With a small data set where
    individual values matter, a dotplot. With hundreds of observations, or
    when you intend to work with percentages, a histogram.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 7 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-choose-graph">
      <statement>
        <p>A researcher has reaction times for <m>12</m> participants and
        wants readers to be able to see each individual value, including the
        exact fastest and slowest times. Which graph should she use?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>A dotplot, since individual values stay visible and the exact
            range can be read off.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. With only <m>12</m> values there is no crowding
            problem, and binning would hide exactly what she wants to
            show.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>A histogram, since it is the more standard graph.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>A histogram would group her twelve values into a handful of
            bars, hiding the individual times and leaving the exact range
            unreadable.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>A histogram with a very small bin width, so each value gets its
            own bar.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>At that point you have built a dotplot the hard way, with a
            graph that invites readers to misjudge the empty bins.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Either one, since both display the same information.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>They do not. A histogram discards individual values by design,
            which is precisely what she needs to avoid here.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 8 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-exact-range">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>You can read the exact overall range of a data set from its
        histogram.</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>False. A histogram tells you only which bin the smallest and
        largest values fall into. In
        <xref ref="fig-nonsmokers"/> we can say the lightest baby weighed
        somewhere between <m>1000</m> and <m>1500</m> grams, but not exactly
        how much. A dotplot shows the actual values, so the range can be
        computed exactly.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <conclusion>
    <title>Summary</title>
    <p>To compare the distributions of a quantitative variable for two groups,
    draw histograms on identical horizontal scales, with identical bin widths
    and identical vertical scales. Describe the shape, center, and spread of
    each, note any outliers, and<mdash/>most importantly<mdash/>say explicitly
    how the groups differ.</p>
    <p>Use percentages rather than counts whenever the groups are different
    sizes. A benchmark value drawn from outside the data, such as a medical
    threshold, makes a comparison concrete and hard to dispute.</p>
    <p>Finally, describe what the data shows and no more. An observational
    study can establish that two things are associated. It cannot, on its own,
    establish that one causes the other.</p>
    <p>Adapted from <pubtitle>Concepts in Statistics</pubtitle>
    (SUNY / Lumen Learning), CC BY 4.0. Birth weight data from Hosmer and
    Lemeshow, collected at Baystate Medical Center, Springfield,
    Massachusetts, <m>1986</m>.</p>
  </conclusion>
</section>